VT_TALK("sql/sql-window", {
 "over": "GROUP BY folds many rows into one answer per group. Sometimes you want the group answer next to every row instead, such as each employee beside the average salary of their department. That is what a window function does. You write an ordinary function, like AVG, and add the word OVER with brackets. Inside the brackets, PARTITION BY says how to split the rows into groups, and every row keeps its own line. With empty brackets the window is the whole table. Now you can compare each person with their own group in a single query, which plain GROUP BY cannot do. Think of a class photo where each child's height is written next to the class average.",
 "rank": "Put ORDER BY inside the OVER brackets and the function can number or rank rows. ROW_NUMBER gives one, two, three, four, even when two salaries tie. RANK gives tied rows the same rank and then skips, so you get one, two, two, four, like positions in a race. DENSE_RANK also shares the rank but does not skip, giving one, two, two, three. Add PARTITION BY and the numbering restarts in each department. In the animation, pick each function and watch Pip light up the rows it compares for every employee. Joseph and Kiran earn the same, so watch what happens to them. Think of a sports day where two runners cross the line together.",
 "offset": "With ORDER BY inside OVER, SUM turns into a running total. For each row it adds everything from the start of the group up to that row, which is how bank statements show a balance after every transaction. LAG reaches back to the row before, and LEAD looks ahead to the row after, so you can work out the change from yesterday or the time until the next order. The first row has nothing before it, so LAG gives NULL there unless you pass a default. FIRST VALUE and LAST VALUE pick values from the ends of the window. Think of a cricket scoreboard that shows the runs of each over and the total so far.",
 "frames": "The frame is the exact set of rows an aggregate looks at for the current row. With no ORDER BY, it is the whole group. With ORDER BY, the default frame runs from the start of the group to the current row, including any rows tied with it. You can set your own, such as ROWS BETWEEN one preceding and one following, which gives a three row moving average that smooths out jumps. The WINDOW clause lets you name a window once and reuse it. One trap catches everyone. Window functions are computed after WHERE, so you cannot filter on them there. Rank inside a subquery or CTE, then filter outside. Think of a magnifying glass that slides along a line of numbers.",
 "levels": "Here is the ladder for window functions. For a beginner, a window function writes a group answer next to every row without hiding any rows. At college level, the OVER clause defines the window with partition, order and frame, and these functions run after grouping. At masters level, frames come in rows, range and groups, and an engine sorts once and sweeps through the rows keeping a running state. At research level, clever structures such as segment trees compute any frame quickly, and some engines add a QUALIFY clause to filter window results directly. Think of a teacher who writes each student's rank on their own report card."
});
